Walk the superclass chain from the concrete type and stop at the first registered handler:

```java
if (error == null) return INTERNAL;
for (Class<?> c = error.getClass(); c != null; c = c.getSuperclass()) {
    var handler = handlers.get(c);
    if (handler != null) return handler.apply(error);
}
return INTERNAL;
```

Checking `handlers.get(error.getClass())` alone would miss subclasses; iterating the map and taking the first `isInstance` match would depend on map order and might choose `Exception` over `NotFoundException`.
